Description: Fix PPP connection drop caused by systemd udev cgroup cleanup
 Since systemd >= 209 (Debian 8 / Ubuntu 16+), all processes spawned during
 udev event processing share the udev worker cgroup.  When the worker becomes
 idle, systemd executes "Cleanup idle workers" and sends SIGKILL to every
 process in the cgroup -- including pppd, which had forked to background but
 remained in the same cgroup.  This caused the PPP connection to drop
 immediately after reaching CTRL_STATE_CONNECTED.
 .
 Fix (two-part):
 .
 1. synce-udev-serial.in: replace the bare background '&' with
    systemd-run --no-block so the Python event handler executes in its own
    transient service unit (and therefore its own cgroup), entirely outside
    the udev worker cgroup.
 .
 2. synceconnector.py.in (serial_start_device): wrap pppd with
    systemd-run --no-block --unit=synce-pppd-<linkname> so pppd is the
    main process of its own transient service unit with an independent
    cgroup that is never subject to udev cleanup.  The nodetach option
    keeps pppd in the foreground as the unit's main process.  A poll loop
    on /var/run/ppp-<linkname>.pid (30 s timeout) replaces the old
    communicate() wait that relied on pppd forking to background.
